 & 2\", 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n   DN200, \"1 & 2\", 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n   DN250, 1, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n   DN250, 2, 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n   DN300, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n   DN300, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n   DN350, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n   DN350, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n   DN400, 1, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n   DN400, 2, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n   DN450, 1, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n   DN450, 2, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n   DN500, 1, 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n   DN500, 2, 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n   DN600, 1, 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n   DN600, 2, 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\n   That's 27 rows.\n\n   Wait — DN500 battery: Q1=100, ratio 40 → Q3/Q1 = 4000/100 = 40. ✓. DN600 battery class 1: Q1=157.5, 6300/157.5 = 40 ✓.\n\nAlso, each flow table has accompanying note text: \"Please note that the flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: - Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013. - Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6. - Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and the ratio Q4/Q3 = 1,25. - The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\"\n\nThis is a note/footnote attached to the tables. I'll add it to the description/footnotes of those matrix tables. In footnotes array with marker like \"note\". The schema footnotes[] at top level has marker/text. I can add to the table's footnotes: e.g. marker \"note\" text full sentence. Or in description. I'll put the note in the table's description field and also footnotes maybe. Description is fine.\n\nmodel_family.models: 15 entries, model_id \"DN40\"..., label maybe \"Reduced bore DN40\". attributes: {\"accuracy_classes\": [1,2], \"power_supply\": [\"AC mains\", \"Battery / Renewable energy\"]}. For sizes where classes are combined (\"1 & 2\") it's still both classes.\n\nShould models also distinguish power variants? Attributes listing both power options covers it.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level:\n- permanent_flow_q3 (attribute name maybe \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\"): unit \"m³/h\", values per size (15).\n- overload_flow_q4: same.\n\nShould I also add accuracy class as model_level? It's in model attributes already.\n\nTest reports: none in fragment. Revision history: none with dates. Footnotes top-level: the [1] footnote from Table 4. I'll include it in top-level footnotes: marker \"1\", text as given. Also table-level footnotes for matrix_tables — the schema shows footnotes per matrix table too.\n\nAlso note the software version/checksum footnote references \"every 10 minutes\" — include.\n\nNow, about \"type_designations\": The AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z is a variant of the family. Actually, hmm: Table 4 title says \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" — likely the FEW45Y.Z is a specific variant (maybe the \"Full functionality\"?) while the family covers many sizes. Since this is fragment 2, part 1 likely lists full type designations. I'll include certified_type with type_designations [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"] and note category \"Water meter\".\n\nLet me also consider components: not named here. Skip.\n\nNow write the JSON. Keep values numeric with dot decimals.\n\nFor matrix table rows, keep numbers as numbers, \"1 & 2\" for accuracy class as string \"1 & 2\" or \"1&2\"? Use \"1 & 2\". Or should class be numeric list? Row value per column single cell — string \"1 & 2\" is faithful.\n\nFor the software tables, remarks \"-\" → null.\n\nTop-level structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"4076767\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"],\n    \"description\": \"Family of reduced bore water meters, meter sizes DN40 to DN600, accuracy class 1 and 2, AC mains powered or battery / renewable energy powered.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [15 entries]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"bore\": {\"value\": \"Reduced bore\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [q3, q4]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [4 entries],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"footnotes\": [\n    {\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nAttribute naming: use canonical snake_case: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" and \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". The rules example: \"Maximum capacity (Emax)\" → maximum_capacity. Table headers: \"Permanent Q3\", \"Overload Q4\", \"Minimum Q1\", \"Transitional Q2\", \"Ratio Q3/Q1\". Canonical: permanent_flow_q3, overload_flow_q4, minimum_flow_q1, transitional_flow_q2, ratio_q3_q1. Good.\n\nFor model_level entries, each value: {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 25} — the schema example shows value objects with footnote markers optional. In R137 example, values are plain numbers inside \"value\". The strict structured value shape (rule 2) applies to characteristics values; but the example for model_level in rule 4 shows {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25}. Hmm — in the R137 example, model_level values are plain numbers: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}. But rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. The examples in rule 4 contradict slightly. To be safe, follow the rule-4 example format: {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 25} — but then unit at attribute level: \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\" at the attribute level as shown in the R137 example ({\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}). Yes, in that example unit_symbol is per-attribute, values have plain numbers. I'll follow that: attribute-level unit_symbol, values plain numbers. That matches the example exactly. But rule 2's structured shape... The example is authoritative for model_level. I'll use: {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 25}, ...]}. Maybe also include \"unit_id\": null at attribute level for consistency. The example didn't include unit_id. I'll include unit_id: null for the caller. Hmm, example lacks it; adding null is harmless.\n\nActually, to maximize compatibility, I'll include both unit_symbol and unit_id: null at attribute level, and values with \"model\" and \"value\" only (numbers). For type_level, use full structured shape.\n\nMatrix table rows: values as numbers.\n\nNow models array:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"label\": \"DN40 reduced bore\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2], \"power_supply_options\": [\"AC mains\", \"Battery / Renewable energy\"]}}\n\nHmm label maybe \"DN40\". Keep attributes concise. Actually power options apply to all sizes (both tables cover all sizes). accuracy classes: DN40 both classes, ..., all sizes have both classes (some as \"1 & 2\" combined). So all models have accuracy_classes [1,2] and both power options. Fine.\n\nAlternatively model_id \"DN40\" with attributes {\"permanent_flow_q3\": 25 ...} — no, that's in model_level.\n\nI'll write models with just model_id and maybe label. The schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. Include attributes with accuracy_classes and power_supply_options.\n\nNow matrix_tables entries:\n\nTable A: name \"Software identification (pages 5–6)\". Hmm the first tables aren't labelled \"Table N\" — the one labeled is \"Table 4 Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". The earlier ones presumably are Tables 1-3 (split). I'll name it \"Software identification\" with description noting part codes.\n\nColumns: [\"software_part_code\", \"functionality\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]. Rows: 12.\n\nTable B: \"Table 4 — Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". columns: [\"software_group\", \"mcu\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"]. rows 14. footnotes: [{\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"...\"}].\n\nTable C: \"Table 5 — General characteristics of the family of instruments: Reduced bore, AC mains powered\". columns [\"meter_size\",\"accuracy_class\",\"minimum_flow_q1\",\"transitional_flow_q2\",\"permanent_flow_q3\",\"overload_flow_q4\",\"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units [null,null,\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null]. description: the note about freely chosen flow rates. rows 24.\n\nTable D: Table 6 same with battery. rows 27.\n\nAlso include the note text in description for both C and D.\n\nThe note: \"Please note that the flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013. Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6. Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and the ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25. The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\" (convert 1,6 → 1.6 and 1,25 → 1.25 in text since it's numeric; the text is prose — keep as \"1,6\"? Rule: decimal point. In prose, I'd convert to 1.6 for consistency.)\n\nNow also: should the fragment include \"components\"? No.\n\nrecommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nLet me now carefully double-check the parsing of all numbers in the tables.\n\nTable 5 (AC mains):\n- DN40 class 1: Q1=0,05→0.05; Q2=0,08→0.08; Q3=25; Q4=31,25→31.25; ratio 500 ✓\n- DN40 class 2: 0,025→0.025; 0,04→0.04; 25; 31.25; 1000 ✓\n- DN50 class 1: 0.08; 0.128; 40; 50; 500 ✓\n- DN50 class 2: 0.04; 0.064; 40; 50; 1000 ✓\n- DN65 \"1 & 2\": 0.063; 0.1008; 63; 78.75; 1000 ✓\n- DN80 \"1 & 2\": 0.1; 0.16; 100